1
00:00:34,031 --> 00:00:39,077
Our planet's
continents are fringed by shallow seas

2
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:43,604
Rarely more than
200 metres deep

3
00:00:43,644 --> 00:00:46,007
they lie on the
continental shelves

4
00:00:46,047 --> 00:00:49,292
which may stretch
sometimes for hundreds of miles

5
00:00:49,332 --> 00:00:52,216
Before the sea
floor drops into deeper

6
00:00:52,256 --> 00:00:53,857
darker waters

7
00:00:59,647 --> 00:01:00,407
All together

8
00:01:00,447 --> 00:01:04,490
they constitute a
mere eight percent of the world's oceans

9
00:01:04,530 --> 00:01:08,573
But they contain
the vast majority of its marine life

10
00:01:43,880 --> 00:01:49,603
A male humpback
whale sings to attract a mate

11
00:02:10,855 --> 00:02:14,938
The whales have
just returned to their breeding grounds

12
00:02:14,978 --> 00:02:17,940
In the shallow
seas of the tropics

13
00:02:25,068 --> 00:02:29,112
The calf is no
more than a few weeks old

14
00:02:34,599 --> 00:02:39,124
Despite being three metres long and weighing nearly a ton

15
00:02:39,245 --> 00:02:42,007
he is nonetheless vulnerable

16
00:02:46,115 --> 00:02:48,357
But his mother
watches over him

17
00:02:48,397 --> 00:02:50,799
And as he
begins to tire

18
00:02:50,839 --> 00:02:53,561
She supports him
close to the surface

19
00:02:53,601 --> 00:02:56,323
So that he can
breathe more easily

20
00:03:20,448 --> 00:03:23,131
These shallows
around the equator

21
00:03:23,171 --> 00:03:25,292
are excellent nurseries

22
00:03:25,453 --> 00:03:27,615
They're warm， calm

23
00:03:27,655 --> 00:03:30,337
and contain
very few predators

24
00:03:33,271 --> 00:03:38,555
The playful calf
is now drinking500 litres of milk a day

25
00:03:38,876 --> 00:03:41,398
But his
mother must starve

26
00:03:41,679 --> 00:03:45,121
There is nothing
for her to eat here

27
00:03:52,392 --> 00:03:54,952
Like many
tropical shallow seas

28
00:03:54,992 --> 00:03:58,553
these crystal
clear waters are virtually lifeless

29
00:03:58,593 --> 00:04:00,994
They receive
year round sunlight

30
00:04:01,034 --> 00:04:04,954
but they lack the
nutrients essential for the growth of plankton

31
00:04:05,875 --> 00:04:09,075
The mother will
be trapped here for the next five months

32
00:04:09,115 --> 00:04:11,436
until her calf
is strong enough

33
00:04:11,476 --> 00:04:13,997
to make the
journey to the feeding grounds

34
00:04:14,037 --> 00:04:15,397
near the poles

35
00:04:39,468 --> 00:04:43,868
Coral reefs are
oases in a watery desert

36
00:04:44,924 --> 00:04:47,804
Most tropical
shallows are barren

37
00:04:47,844 --> 00:04:51,084
But these coral
havens contain one quarter

38
00:04:51,124 --> 00:04:53,844
Of all the marine
life on our planet

39
00:05:13,656 --> 00:05:16,020
Reefs are the
work of polyps

40
00:05:16,060 --> 00:05:20,185
tiny colonial
animals like minute sea anemones

41
00:05:20,387 --> 00:05:24,033
Yet the Great
Barrier Reef is so big

42
00:05:24,073 --> 00:05:26,275
it can be seen
from the moon

43
00:05:32,606 --> 00:05:35,968
It's actually 2
000 separate reefs

44
00:05:36,008 --> 00:05:37,929
that together
form a barrier

45
00:05:37,969 --> 00:05:40,610
stretching for
over a thousand miles

46
00:05:40,650 --> 00:05:43,691
along Australia'
s north eastern coast

47
00:05:47,133 --> 00:05:49,494
Despite
its vast size

48
00:05:49,535 --> 00:05:52,696
this reef does
not contain the greatest variety

49
00:05:52,736 --> 00:05:54,937
of marine life
on the planet

50
00:05:56,478 --> 00:05:57,360
For that

51
00:05:57,400 --> 00:05:59,042
one must
travel north

52
00:05:59,082 --> 00:06:00,683
to Indonesia

53
00:06:28,228 --> 00:06:31,429
There are
individual reefs in Indonesia

54
00:06:31,469 --> 00:06:34,311
that contain
almost as many kinds of fish

55
00:06:34,351 --> 00:06:36,992
as live in the
whole of the Caribbean

56
00:06:38,873 --> 00:06:42,794
There are also
ten times the number of coral species

57
00:06:45,985 --> 00:06:48,311
Coral thrive
in these waters

58
00:06:48,351 --> 00:06:50,636
With the help of
microscopic plants

59
00:06:50,676 --> 00:06:51,598
algae

60
00:06:51,638 --> 00:06:54,923
That grow within
the tissues of the polyps

61
00:07:01,048 --> 00:07:06,373
And the polyps feed by snaring passing morsels with their tentacles

62
00:07:18,912 --> 00:07:19,751
At night

63
00:07:19,791 --> 00:07:21,751
the algae
are inactive

64
00:07:21,791 --> 00:07:25,391
But then the
polyps put out even more tentacles

65
00:07:25,431 --> 00:07:26,231
So coral

66
00:07:26,271 --> 00:07:27,151
in effect

67
00:07:27,191 --> 00:07:29,191
feeds
around the clock

68
00:07:47,162 --> 00:07:49,842
This wellbalanced alliance

69
00:07:49,882 --> 00:07:53,563
brings benefits
to both polyps and algae

70
00:07:53,603 --> 00:07:54,604
And between them

71
00:07:54,644 --> 00:07:58,484
they turn the
barren seas into rich gardens

72
00:08:09,210 --> 00:08:13,332
The Indonesian
reefs contain such a variety of life

73
00:08:13,372 --> 00:08:16,493
because they lie
at a giant crossroads

74
00:08:16,653 --> 00:08:19,974
This is the
meeting place for different seas

75
00:08:20,014 --> 00:08:22,895
The Indian Ocean
and the Pacific

76
00:08:25,872 --> 00:08:26,472
Here

77
00:08:26,512 --> 00:08:29,232
everything
demands a closer look

78
00:08:29,552 --> 00:08:31,512
On the surface
of this sea fan

79
00:08:31,552 --> 00:08:34,952
there are two
polyps that are not polyps

80
00:08:35,513 --> 00:08:37,593
They're
pygmy seahorses

81
00:08:37,633 --> 00:08:39,393
the world'
s smallest

82
00:08:39,433 --> 00:08:41,913
less than two
centimetres high

83
00:08:54,655 --> 00:08:58,738
They are males
settling a territorial dispute

84
00:08:58,778 --> 00:09:00,699
by headbutting

85
00:09:09,705 --> 00:09:11,906
An electric flash？

86
00:09:13,308 --> 00:09:14,068
No

87
00:09:14,108 --> 00:09:17,070
the display
of a file clam

88
00:09:22,263 --> 00:09:26,672
Perhaps this extraordinary pulsation of the clam's fleshy mantle

89
00:09:26,712 --> 00:09:29,957
is a warning to
frighten away nibbling fish

90
00:09:30,239 --> 00:09:32,602
But no one
really knows

91
00:09:47,362 --> 00:09:49,801
And there are
snakes here too

92
00:09:50,521 --> 00:09:51,961
lots of them

93
00:09:57,560 --> 00:10:01,039
These are
banded sea crets

94
00:10:01,359 --> 00:10:03,398
They lay
their eggs on land

95
00:10:03,438 --> 00:10:06,438
but they hunt
here in the water

96
00:10:11,168 --> 00:10:15,171
They're too slow
to catch fish in a straight chase

97
00:10:15,212 --> 00:10:19,895
So they seek prey
that is hiding in the nooks and crannies of the coral

98
00:10:22,418 --> 00:10:26,741
Their bite is highly venomous and paralyzes their victims

99
00:10:31,946 --> 00:10:33,387
And on this reef

100
00:10:33,427 --> 00:10:36,589
the snakes do
not hunt alone

101
00:10:42,526 --> 00:10:45,887
Schools of yellow
goatfish and trevally

102
00:10:45,927 --> 00:10:47,967
are seeking
similar prey

103
00:10:48,007 --> 00:10:51,008
and they attract
the snake's attention

104
00:10:51,048 --> 00:10:53,889
As one group of
hunters searches the reef

105
00:10:54,009 --> 00:10:55,929
they're
joined by the other

106
00:11:04,652 --> 00:11:08,933
At least 30
snakes have now joined the caravan

107
00:11:15,171 --> 00:11:18,695
The big fish
scare the prey into cracks

108
00:11:18,735 --> 00:11:21,698
And there the
snakes can catch them

109
00:11:22,139 --> 00:11:24,182
And anything
fleeing from the crates

110
00:11:24,222 --> 00:11:27,865
Will swim straight into the mouths of the waiting trevally

111
00:11:29,187 --> 00:11:31,069
There's
nowhere to hide

112
00:11:36,954 --> 00:11:39,475
As the raiders
scour the reef

113
00:11:39,796 --> 00:11:42,677
More and more
snakes join the hunt

114
00:12:04,968 --> 00:12:08,288
This cooperation
between snakes and fish

115
00:12:08,328 --> 00:12:10,168
spectacular
though it is

116
00:12:10,208 --> 00:12:12,368
has only
recently been observed

117
00:12:12,408 --> 00:12:16,807
for it only happens on the most remote reefs in Indonesia

118
00:12:26,499 --> 00:12:30,464
Perhaps such hunting alliances were once a common sight

119
00:12:30,504 --> 00:12:31,265
But today

120
00:12:31,305 --> 00:12:34,510
no more than six
percent of Indonesia's reefs

121
00:12:34,550 --> 00:12:36,672
are in their
pristine state

122
00:13:05,125 --> 00:13:09,447
Beyond the coral
stretches a world of shifting sand

123
00:13:10,463 --> 00:13:12,588
Out there with
nowhere to hide

124
00:13:12,628 --> 00:13:14,592
survival
is not easy

125
00:13:14,632 --> 00:13:17,356
and camouflage
can be crucial

126
00:13:20,163 --> 00:13:21,846
If this
wasn't moving

127
00:13:21,886 --> 00:13:24,652
you might think
it was a shell or a rock

128
00:13:24,692 --> 00:13:25,493
In fact

129
00:13:25,533 --> 00:13:27,015
it's an octopus

130
00:13:29,110 --> 00:13:30,273
A gurnard

131
00:13:30,313 --> 00:13:33,878
its huge pectoral fins disguise its shape

132
00:13:34,160 --> 00:13:38,528
And they can also
help in clearing away sand when searching for food

133
00:13:43,178 --> 00:13:46,303
The jawfish
hides underground

134
00:13:48,584 --> 00:13:51,150
The wonderpus
octopus on the other hand

135
00:13:51,190 --> 00:13:52,834
has such a
powerful bite

136
00:13:52,874 --> 00:13:55,079
That it has a
special warning display

137
00:13:55,119 --> 00:13:57,643
To tell others to
keep out of its way

138
00:14:10,021 --> 00:14:10,982
Here and there

139
00:14:11,022 --> 00:14:13,624
plants
manage to take root

140
00:14:14,105 --> 00:14:17,027
And they're
cropped by green turtles

141
00:14:17,548 --> 00:14:22,512
Seagrasses are the only flowering plants that have managed to grow in the sea

142
00:14:22,833 --> 00:14:25,436
Although they put
out a few ribbonlike leaves

143
00:14:25,476 --> 00:14:28,879
They produce very
extensive networks of fleshy stems

144
00:14:28,919 --> 00:14:30,040
rhizomes

145
00:14:30,080 --> 00:14:32,122
that are
buried in the sand

146
00:14:39,435 --> 00:14:40,638
At their lushiest

147
00:14:40,678 --> 00:14:42,843
they can
transform the seabed

148
00:14:42,883 --> 00:14:45,167
into an
underwater meadow

149
00:14:46,252 --> 00:14:50,543
The largest expanse grows in the shallow waters of Shark Bay

150
00:14:50,583 --> 00:14:52,425
in Western
Australia

151
00:14:55,078 --> 00:15:01,010
These vast aquatic grasslands stretch for 1500 square miles

152
00:15:01,172 --> 00:15:03,938
And like
terrestrial prairies

153
00:15:03,978 --> 00:15:06,383
they support
herds of grazers

154
00:15:06,945 --> 00:15:08,227
dugongs

155
00:15:22,010 --> 00:15:25,613
Dugongs are the
largest herbivores in the sea

156
00:15:25,734 --> 00:15:28,817
They can be three
metres long and weigh half a ton

157
00:15:28,857 --> 00:15:31,180
And they eat
nothing but sea grass

158
00:15:31,220 --> 00:15:33,182
mostly the
fleshy rhizomes

159
00:15:33,222 --> 00:15:36,465
which they
excavate with their mobile lips

160
00:15:51,760 --> 00:15:56,801
A herd can clear
a patch of sea grass the size of a football pitch

161
00:15:56,841 --> 00:15:58,321
in a single day

162
00:16:22,800 --> 00:16:27,322
Food is not evenly distributed in the tropical shallow seas

163
00:16:27,362 --> 00:16:29,683
and it can take
a lot of finding

164
00:16:29,924 --> 00:16:33,205
But bottlenose
dolphins are inquisitive

165
00:16:33,245 --> 00:16:34,246
energetic

166
00:16:34,286 --> 00:16:36,087
and very
intelligent

167
00:16:36,127 --> 00:16:39,488
And they have
discovered a shoal of bait fish

168
00:16:47,393 --> 00:16:48,073
Together

169
00:16:48,113 --> 00:16:49,793
they ride a wave

170
00:16:49,833 --> 00:16:52,632
using it to carry
them into the shallows

171
00:16:52,672 --> 00:16:53,792
And there

172
00:16:53,832 --> 00:16:56,352
it will be easier
to make the catch

173
00:17:23,457 --> 00:17:25,417
In Western
Australia

174
00:17:25,457 --> 00:17:29,536
these dolphins
have taken on an even tougher challenge

175
00:17:30,536 --> 00:17:33,776
The fish have
taken refuge close to the beach

176
00:17:33,816 --> 00:17:37,135
where the water
is only a few centimetres deep

177
00:17:41,138 --> 00:17:46,662
Tail slapping is
a method dolphins often use to stun their prey

178
00:17:46,782 --> 00:17:49,264
But it doesn't
seem to work here

179
00:17:59,472 --> 00:18:02,514
The fish are
tantalisingly close

180
00:18:02,674 --> 00:18:04,715
But they're
still out of reach

181
00:18:04,916 --> 00:18:08,558
So the dolphins
try another technique

182
00:18:12,956 --> 00:18:15,157
Vigorously
pumping their tails

183
00:18:15,197 --> 00:18:17,398
they work
up some speed

184
00:18:17,878 --> 00:18:18,758
And then

185
00:18:18,798 --> 00:18:20,439
they hydroplane

186
00:18:48,318 --> 00:18:52,437
Their momentum carries them right through the shallowest waters

187
00:18:52,477 --> 00:18:54,516
and onto the fish

188
00:19:05,701 --> 00:19:09,663
Now they're in
real danger of being stranded

189
00:19:09,824 --> 00:19:12,706
But fortune
favours the brave

190
00:19:21,153 --> 00:19:23,355
Younger
dolphins lie alongside

191
00:19:23,395 --> 00:19:24,475
watching

192
00:19:24,556 --> 00:19:27,758
But so far only
eight individuals here

193
00:19:27,798 --> 00:19:30,880
Have mastered
this daring technique

194
00:20:21,666 --> 00:20:24,547
Although most
life in tropical waters

195
00:20:24,587 --> 00:20:27,229
is concentrated
around the coral reefs

196
00:20:27,269 --> 00:20:29,110
and the sea
grass meadows

197
00:20:29,150 --> 00:20:32,711
There are some
spectacular exceptions

198
00:20:38,883 --> 00:20:40,886
The desert
of Bahrain

199
00:20:40,926 --> 00:20:43,089
seems a very
unlikely place

200
00:20:43,129 --> 00:20:46,934
to find a crowded
bustling colony of seabirds

201
00:20:47,455 --> 00:20:48,657
But every year

202
00:20:48,697 --> 00:20:51,622
a hundred
thousand cecotrichomines

203
00:20:51,662 --> 00:20:53,503
gather
here to breed

204
00:21:00,725 --> 00:21:02,526
It's swelteringly hot

205
00:21:02,566 --> 00:21:07,689
And only vigorous
panting can prevent the birds from fatal overheating

206
00:21:08,410 --> 00:21:12,012
This hardly seems
a good place to rear young

207
00:21:14,134 --> 00:21:18,016
But at least
there are no landbased predators here

208
00:21:18,337 --> 00:21:22,019
The only source
of trouble is likely to be the neighbours

209
00:21:22,140 --> 00:21:26,542
So each nest is
built just beyond pecking reach

210
00:21:42,366 --> 00:21:44,487
But what
about food？

211
00:21:44,807 --> 00:21:49,248
There's only bare
sand and the warm shallow sea beyond

212
00:21:49,489 --> 00:21:52,730
Neither seem
likely to produce enough nourishment

213
00:21:52,770 --> 00:21:55,531
To support bird
life on this scale

214
00:21:59,492 --> 00:22:03,013
The answer is
blowing in the wind

215
00:22:05,345 --> 00:22:09,385
Sand whipped up
by Shamal's offshore winds

216
00:22:09,425 --> 00:22:12,665
blows into the
seas of the Arabian Gulf

217
00:22:13,225 --> 00:22:15,345
With the grains
come nutrients

218
00:22:15,385 --> 00:22:17,065
which act
as fertiliser

219
00:22:17,105 --> 00:22:19,585
and they
transform the shallow sea

220
00:22:19,625 --> 00:22:21,945
into a rich
fishing ground

221
00:22:27,897 --> 00:22:29,576
So paradoxically

222
00:22:29,616 --> 00:22:32,056
it's the roasted
sands of Arabia

223
00:22:32,096 --> 00:22:36,616
that prevent the
Gulf from being another desert in the sea

224
00:22:50,069 --> 00:22:53,591
The whale calf is
now five months old

225
00:22:53,791 --> 00:22:55,913
He's almost
doubled in size

226
00:22:55,953 --> 00:22:59,995
And his days in
his tropical nursery are coming to an end

227
00:23:00,436 --> 00:23:04,038
It has been a
warm and safe place in which to grow up

228
00:23:04,079 --> 00:23:07,320
But there's
nothing to eat here for his mother

229
00:23:09,202 --> 00:23:13,885
She has been living off her fat reserves for the last eight months

230
00:23:14,046 --> 00:23:16,487
And she's
close to starving

231
00:23:17,092 --> 00:23:18,251
She must leave now

232
00:23:18,291 --> 00:23:19,891
while she still
has enough energy

233
00:23:19,931 --> 00:23:22,611
to guide and
protect her calf

234
00:23:22,651 --> 00:23:24,771
on the long
voyage ahead

235
00:23:42,671 --> 00:23:44,593
All across
the tropics

236
00:23:44,633 --> 00:23:47,757
humpbacks are
heading away from the equator

237
00:23:47,797 --> 00:23:50,280
towards the
rich temperate seas

238
00:23:50,320 --> 00:23:54,003
of both the southern and the northern hemispheres

239
00:23:56,967 --> 00:23:58,689
These are colder

240
00:23:58,729 --> 00:23:59,570
rougher

241
00:23:59,610 --> 00:24:02,132
and more
dangerous waters

242
00:24:14,403 --> 00:24:17,483
Mother and calf
must stay close

243
00:24:36,607 --> 00:24:41,208
They can send sound signals to one another above the roar of the ocean

244
00:24:41,248 --> 00:24:44,089
by slapping
fins on the surface

245
00:25:05,588 --> 00:25:06,469
In winter

246
00:25:06,509 --> 00:25:11,033
the temperate
seas are lashed by violent storms

247
00:25:29,027 --> 00:25:31,829
The turbulence
stirs the water

248
00:25:31,869 --> 00:25:35,511
and draws
nutrients up from the depths

249
00:25:43,998 --> 00:25:47,600
But nutrients
alone cannot support life

250
00:25:48,482 --> 00:25:51,003
There must
also be sunlight

251
00:25:54,265 --> 00:25:55,265
In the spring

252
00:25:55,305 --> 00:25:58,585
as the sun daily
climbs higher in the sky

253
00:25:58,625 --> 00:26:01,024
the algae
start to grow

254
00:26:01,864 --> 00:26:05,024
Blooms the size
of the Amazon rainforest

255
00:26:05,064 --> 00:26:07,104
turn the
seas green

256
00:26:10,503 --> 00:26:11,383
Individually

257
00:26:11,423 --> 00:26:13,463
the algae are tiny

258
00:26:13,662 --> 00:26:14,622
but together

259
00:26:14,662 --> 00:26:19,822
they produce three quarters of all the oxygen in our atmosphere

260
00:26:23,464 --> 00:26:27,705
They're eaten by
an array of bewildering creatures

261
00:26:27,906 --> 00:26:30,907
Salps appear in
the plankton soup

262
00:26:36,790 --> 00:26:40,112
Individuals link
together to form chains

263
00:26:40,152 --> 00:26:43,153
which can
stretch for 15 metres

264
00:26:53,817 --> 00:26:55,697
Pumping water
through their bodies

265
00:26:55,737 --> 00:26:59,896
they strain out
algae and other edible particles

266
00:27:10,844 --> 00:27:14,243
Comb jellies
cruise through the water

267
00:27:14,283 --> 00:27:17,763
They too flourish
in this seasonal soup

268
00:27:17,803 --> 00:27:19,122
And for
short periods

269
00:27:19,162 --> 00:27:22,202
they appear in
astounding numbers

270
00:27:23,241 --> 00:27:24,161
Krill

271
00:27:24,201 --> 00:27:25,921
shrimplike
creatures

272
00:27:25,961 --> 00:27:26,641
By weight

273
00:27:26,681 --> 00:27:29,360
they're the most
abundant animals on the planet

274
00:27:29,400 --> 00:27:33,679
A single swarm
can contain two million tons of them

275
00:27:42,603 --> 00:27:46,208
And that is a
lot of fish food

276
00:27:55,748 --> 00:28:01,069
The shallow temperate seas support the greatest concentrations of fish on our planet

277
00:28:01,549 --> 00:28:05,669
Huge shoals migrate from their overwintering grounds in the depths

278
00:28:05,709 --> 00:28:08,630
To feed in
these rich waters

279
00:28:17,703 --> 00:28:23,512
It's these shoals
that support most of the world's sea mammals

280
00:28:24,514 --> 00:28:27,960
Sealions have all
the agility and speed needed

281
00:28:28,000 --> 00:28:29,762
to collect
what they want

282
00:28:30,364 --> 00:28:33,367
and seemingly
delight in doing so

283
00:29:08,621 --> 00:29:09,981
Dusky dolphin

284
00:29:10,021 --> 00:29:12,981
often in pods
two hundred strong

285
00:29:13,021 --> 00:29:15,621
Work together to
reap the harvest

286
00:29:18,382 --> 00:29:20,622
They break up the
shoals into smaller

287
00:29:20,662 --> 00:29:22,302
more manageable balls

288
00:29:22,342 --> 00:29:24,742
And all the
hunters benefit

289
00:29:51,903 --> 00:29:53,184
By midsummer

290
00:29:53,224 --> 00:29:57,067
the surface
nutrients have all been absorbed

291
00:29:57,148 --> 00:29:58,869
The algae die

292
00:29:58,909 --> 00:30:01,591
and the food
chain collapses

293
00:30:06,412 --> 00:30:08,014
In a few
special places

294
00:30:08,054 --> 00:30:08,814
however

295
00:30:08,854 --> 00:30:12,338
the temperate
seas sustain these levels of life

296
00:30:12,378 --> 00:30:14,139
throughout
the summer

297
00:30:14,540 --> 00:30:16,582
Along the
coast of California

298
00:30:16,622 --> 00:30:21,187
ocean currents carry a constant supply of nutrients up from the depths

299
00:30:21,227 --> 00:30:23,028
to the
surface layers

300
00:30:23,990 --> 00:30:27,954
These upwellings
fertilise forests of giant kelp

301
00:30:27,994 --> 00:30:30,516
that thrive in
the summer sunshine

302
00:30:37,833 --> 00:30:42,074
The algal towers
are as high as a threestorey house

303
00:30:42,114 --> 00:30:45,515
and they can grow
by half a metre a day

304
00:31:00,188 --> 00:31:01,548
Life in the kelp

305
00:31:01,588 --> 00:31:05,430
is as full of
drama as in any other forest

306
00:31:05,590 --> 00:31:08,431
But the cast
is less familiar

307
00:31:09,111 --> 00:31:13,272
An army of sea
urchins is mounting an attack

308
00:31:15,834 --> 00:31:19,995
The urchin plague
strikes at the kelp's holdfasts

309
00:31:20,035 --> 00:31:22,956
Their crucial
attachments to the rock

310
00:31:37,479 --> 00:31:40,642
Whole fasts are
extremely tough

311
00:31:40,803 --> 00:31:43,447
But each urchin
has five teeth

312
00:31:43,487 --> 00:31:45,009
Which are
selfsharpening

313
00:31:45,049 --> 00:31:47,451
And are replaced
every few months

314
00:31:55,302 --> 00:31:58,905
Urchins felled
vast areas of kelp forest

315
00:31:58,945 --> 00:32:03,067
creating clearings known as urchin barrens

316
00:32:06,670 --> 00:32:07,391
Yet

317
00:32:07,431 --> 00:32:10,593
"barrens" is a
poor description

318
00:32:14,636 --> 00:32:18,599
Millions of
invertebrates invade the seabed

319
00:32:33,901 --> 00:32:37,101
The most fearsome
predator here is a giant

320
00:32:37,542 --> 00:32:41,462
The sunflower
starfish is a metre across

321
00:32:41,502 --> 00:32:44,463
With an appetite
for brittle stars

322
00:33:02,880 --> 00:33:06,921
It uses its feet
to taste for prey

323
00:33:11,842 --> 00:33:13,923
When its
actions are speeded up

324
00:33:13,963 --> 00:33:15,163
it becomes clear

325
00:33:15,203 --> 00:33:18,244
that the predator'
s fondness for the brittle stars

326
00:33:18,284 --> 00:33:21,685
is almost matched
by the brittle star's ability

327
00:33:21,725 --> 00:33:23,405
to get out
of the way

328
00:33:36,041 --> 00:33:37,483
Sand dollars

329
00:33:37,523 --> 00:33:39,044
flat sea urchins

330
00:33:39,084 --> 00:33:41,607
cluster
together as a defence

331
00:33:47,407 --> 00:33:52,166
But it doesn't
seem to work against the sunflower starfish

332
00:33:52,206 --> 00:33:56,766
The predator extrudes its stomach and wraps it around its victims

333
00:33:56,806 --> 00:33:59,125
liquifying
their soft parts

334
00:34:00,325 --> 00:34:03,925
Nothing is left
of them except their white skeletons

335
00:34:11,684 --> 00:34:16,204
The Californian
upwellings are seasonal and relatively small

336
00:34:16,524 --> 00:34:18,045
But in
southern Africa

337
00:34:18,085 --> 00:34:19,325
they're so big

338
00:34:19,365 --> 00:34:22,645
they create seas
rich enough to support colonies

339
00:34:22,685 --> 00:34:25,206
of over a
million seals

340
00:34:30,280 --> 00:34:35,564
The Benguela current sweeps along the western coastline of southern Africa

341
00:34:35,604 --> 00:34:39,407
driving nutrientrich waters up to the surface

342
00:34:41,049 --> 00:34:42,129
And then

343
00:34:42,170 --> 00:34:44,131
at the
southern tip of Africa

344
00:34:44,171 --> 00:34:46,413
it meets the
Agulhas current

345
00:34:46,453 --> 00:34:48,334
arriving
from the east

346
00:34:48,615 --> 00:34:51,817
The result：
even richer waters

347
00:35:03,912 --> 00:35:09,514
The seals here
thrive on a diet of fish and squid

348
00:35:11,155 --> 00:35:12,675
In temperate seas

349
00:35:12,715 --> 00:35:16,197
there may actually be more squid than fish

350
00:35:23,824 --> 00:35:26,105
These are
chocca squid

351
00:35:26,145 --> 00:35:28,146
And they lay
their egg capsules

352
00:35:28,186 --> 00:35:33,268
In sandy shallows
bathed by the warmer Agulhas current

353
00:35:41,992 --> 00:35:46,393
Each capsule
contains a hundred tiny squid

354
00:35:51,787 --> 00:35:52,789
Within a few days

355
00:35:52,829 --> 00:35:55,152
they develop
spots of pigment，which

356
00:35:55,192 --> 00:35:56,314
when they're adult

357
00:35:56,354 --> 00:35:59,317
they will use to
communicate with one another

358
00:36:12,055 --> 00:36:14,934
With females
continuing to lay eggs

359
00:36:14,974 --> 00:36:18,893
and males still
preoccupied with repelling rivals

360
00:36:18,933 --> 00:36:21,813
the squid
drop their guard

361
00:36:25,692 --> 00:36:27,172
Stingray

362
00:36:34,970 --> 00:36:38,930
Shorttailed stingray can be up to two metres across

363
00:36:45,494 --> 00:36:48,215
They're the
largest of all the stingrays

364
00:36:48,255 --> 00:36:51,016
and they have
appetites to match

365
00:37:20,230 --> 00:37:23,272
Another predator
is on the prowl

366
00:37:25,476 --> 00:37:29,039
The aptly named
ragged tooth shark

367
00:37:33,437 --> 00:37:35,717
Raggies grow to
three metres long

368
00:37:35,757 --> 00:37:38,078
But they share
these waters with a shark

369
00:37:38,118 --> 00:37:39,838
twice their size

370
00:37:57,324 --> 00:37:58,604
The great white

371
00:38:01,510 --> 00:38:04,910
The largest
predatory fish on the planet

372
00:38:11,951 --> 00:38:13,231
Each dawn

373
00:38:13,271 --> 00:38:17,391
Cape fur seals
leave their colony to go fishing

374
00:38:18,311 --> 00:38:19,951
To reach
the open sea

375
00:38:19,991 --> 00:38:22,991
they must cross a
narrow strip of water

376
00:38:23,391 --> 00:38:27,152
And that is
patrolled by great whites

377
00:38:36,905 --> 00:38:40,268
Each seal is
indeed swimming for its life

378
00:39:04,150 --> 00:39:07,270
The shark
relies on surprise

379
00:40:08,539 --> 00:40:13,942
The great white's
turn of speed is powered by a high metabolism

380
00:40:13,982 --> 00:40:17,024
They only thrive
in cold temperate seas

381
00:40:17,064 --> 00:40:20,866
For only these
waters contain sufficient food necessary

382
00:40:20,906 --> 00:40:23,867
To fuel such a
ravenous predator

383
00:40:56,568 --> 00:40:58,888
As you travel
towards the poles

384
00:40:58,928 --> 00:41:00,488
North or South

385
00:41:00,528 --> 00:41:05,049
The colder stormier seas can become even richer

386
00:41:07,249 --> 00:41:10,930
Midway between
South Africa and the South Pole

387
00:41:11,210 --> 00:41:14,650
Lies the isolated
island of Marion

388
00:41:20,636 --> 00:41:24,684
The island sits
in the infamous Roaring Forties

389
00:41:24,724 --> 00:41:27,249
Where incessant
gale force winds

390
00:41:27,289 --> 00:41:29,774
Draw nutrients
up from the depths

391
00:41:29,814 --> 00:41:33,420
Ensuring plenty
of food for king penguins

392
00:41:36,498 --> 00:41:39,946
The kings are
returning from a threeday fishing trip

393
00:41:39,986 --> 00:41:41,989
With food for
their chicks

394
00:41:42,110 --> 00:41:42,912
But first

395
00:41:42,952 --> 00:41:45,398
they must cross
a crowded beach

396
00:41:45,438 --> 00:41:48,003
Threading their
way between gigantic

397
00:41:48,043 --> 00:41:50,928
and badtempered
elephant seals

398
00:42:02,584 --> 00:42:05,706
The 200，000
penguins breeding here

399
00:42:05,746 --> 00:42:09,148
are testament to
the richness of the fishing

400
00:42:13,351 --> 00:42:17,194
King chicks are
dependent on their mothers for over a year

401
00:42:17,234 --> 00:42:20,956
And this puts a
great deal of pressure on the parents

402
00:42:35,712 --> 00:42:37,072
Being flightless

403
00:42:37,112 --> 00:42:42,033
the returning penguins must cross the open beach on foot

404
00:43:14,898 --> 00:43:19,584
Fur seals that have come to the beach to breathe are waiting for them

405
00:43:27,981 --> 00:43:30,822
Fur seals
normally live on krill

406
00:43:30,902 --> 00:43:31,903
但係呢啲已經有咗，一個唔預期嘅口味

407
00:43:31,943 --> 00:43:32,823
係食脂膶嘅鯨魚

408
00:44:29,558 --> 00:44:32,561
Penguins may be
featherweights by comparison

409
00:44:32,601 --> 00:44:36,845
But they have
razor sharp bills and a feisty character

410
00:44:39,923 --> 00:44:42,405
The seal could
easily lose an eye

411
00:44:42,445 --> 00:44:45,807
The only safe way
to grab a penguin is from behind

412
00:44:46,207 --> 00:44:48,608
And the birds are
well aware of that

413
00:45:11,218 --> 00:45:14,182
Both animals are
clumsy on this terrain

414
00:45:14,222 --> 00:45:16,905
But the penguin
has the more to lose

415
00:45:29,436 --> 00:45:30,516
Made it

416
00:45:31,838 --> 00:45:35,200
Two out of three
penguins survive the seal attacks

417
00:45:35,240 --> 00:45:38,682
and succeed in
reaching their ever hungry chicks

418
00:46:09,465 --> 00:46:14,066
The humpbacks are
nearing the end of their epic journeys

419
00:46:19,466 --> 00:46:22,506
After two months
and thousands of miles

420
00:46:22,546 --> 00:46:25,307
they're
entering the polar seas

421
00:46:25,347 --> 00:46:28,147
both in the
north and the south

422
00:46:39,270 --> 00:46:40,878
In the far north

423
00:46:40,918 --> 00:46:43,367
winter is
over at last

424
00:46:43,410 --> 00:46:45,980
and the ice is
starting to melt

425
00:46:50,107 --> 00:46:53,710
The Ilulissat
Island chain running west from Alaska

426
00:46:53,750 --> 00:46:56,151
is the gateway
to the Bering Sea

427
00:46:56,832 --> 00:46:58,873
With the
retreating ice

428
00:46:58,913 --> 00:47:03,636
rough weather and
ferocious currents stir up these shallow seas

429
00:47:03,797 --> 00:47:05,118
Add sunshine

430
00:47:05,158 --> 00:47:08,800
and the mix is
spectacularly productive

431
00:47:18,921 --> 00:47:25,368
Five million shearwaters have flown almost ten thousand miles from Australia to get here

432
00:47:26,249 --> 00:47:27,250
In all

433
00:47:27,410 --> 00:47:29,893
80 million seabirds come here for the summer

434
00:47:29,933 --> 00:47:34,457
The greatest concentration to be found anywhere on Earth

435
00:47:47,816 --> 00:47:51,220
The humpbacks
have finally arrived

436
00:48:10,603 --> 00:48:14,843
The giant shearwater flocks hunt the krill swarms

437
00:48:14,883 --> 00:48:19,363
Sometimes diving
to depths of 40 metres to reach them

438
00:48:43,149 --> 00:48:48,232
A large humpback
eats three tons of krill a day

439
00:49:00,882 --> 00:49:02,884
The polar
seas in summer

440
00:49:02,924 --> 00:49:05,406
are the most
productive on the planet

441
00:49:05,446 --> 00:49:09,008
And the whales
gorge themselves round the clock

442
00:49:16,302 --> 00:49:18,661
The fat reserves
they lay down now

443
00:49:18,701 --> 00:49:21,541
will keep them
alive during the year to come

444
00:49:23,141 --> 00:49:25,820
But it may not
always be this way

445
00:49:25,980 --> 00:49:29,500
Fish and krill
stocks are declining so rapidly

446
00:49:29,540 --> 00:49:33,899
That spectacles
like this may soon be part of history

447
00:49:43,685 --> 00:49:47,765
Once the mother
and calf have reached their feeding grounds

448
00:49:47,805 --> 00:49:49,405
they will separate

449
00:49:52,724 --> 00:49:53,645
With luck

450
00:49:53,685 --> 00:49:57,167
the calf will
make the epic journey across the oceans

451
00:49:57,207 --> 00:49:59,128
from equator to pole

452
00:49:59,168 --> 00:50:01,489
another 70 times

453
00:50:01,929 --> 00:50:05,531
Cruising back and
forth between the shallow seas

454
00:50:05,891 --> 00:50:08,933
Where life
proliferates so abundantly

455
00:50:08,973 --> 00:50:10,373
on our planet

